{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nf5fm42istlqoknhpao7nibegiii6npoegqzzzkok7x7ln5yqkqq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **The Rust translation is byte-identical to the C** — no divergence found, so no fixes to `src/lib.rs` were needed.\n\n## What the library is\n\n86 lines of C (a CWE-369 divide-by-zero test case) exporting 5 symbols. Everything returns `void` and emits output via libc `printf`, so \"output\" means bytes written to fd 1. The harness captures them by `dup2`-ing a temp file over fd 1 — which works for both libraries because the Rust translation deliberately imports `printf` rather than using `std::io::stdout`.\n\n## Results\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — symbol diff | **empty** (5/5 exact-name matches) |\n| Phase B — `CONFIGS.md` | **28/28 rows** pass, randomized (fixed seed) |\n| Phase C — `ERRORS.md` | **13/13 rows** pass |\n| All feature combinations | **6/6** profile × feature combos, 48 tests each |\n\n`Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` and `lib.rs` has no `#[cfg]` gates, so there's one real configuration; I verified all flag spellings and both profiles anyway.\n\n## The part that mattered most\n\nGreen tests prove nothing unless the harness can *detect* divergence, so I injected 20 deliberate bugs as a negative control. **This caught two harness defects that made the entire suite pass vacuously** — invisible from test output alone:\n\n1. `ensure_rust_so()` returned early when the `.so` existed. `cargo test` builds the crate as an *rlib* for test binaries and **never refreshes the cdylib**, so I was testing a stale artifact. My first mutation escaped because of this.\n2. After forcing a rebuild, the inner `cargo build` still shared the target dir with the outer `cargo test`. Cargo's fingerprints are mtime-based, so when `src/lib.rs` was rewritten in the same wall-clock second as the previous build, cargo judged it fresh and kept the old `.so`.\n\nFixed by building into a dedicated, wiped `target/difftest-cdylib` with an assertion that the artifact isn't older than the source. After the fix: **16/16 non-equivalent mutants caught**, including a one-character typo in the message, argument swaps, call-order changes, and a removed `#[no_mangle]`.\n\nThree escapes are *equivalent mutants*, not gaps — I proved each indistinguishable by enumerating **all 2^32 `f32` bit patterns** (0 differing inputs). E.g. `f32`-vs-`f64` threshold comparison is unobservable because no `f32` lands between `9.99999997475e-07` and `1e-6`.\n\n## Behavioral subtlety confirmed\n\n`goodB2G` compares `fabs((double)data) > 0.000001` where the literal is a **double** but `data` is a **float**. The `float` nearest `1e-6` is `9.99999997475e-07`, which is *below* the threshold — so `good(1e-6f)` takes the rejection branch, while one ULP up prints `99999988`. Both implementations agree. My first draft asserted `1000000` here (I'd mentally computed `100/1e-6` as `1e6` instead of `1e8`); the C was right and I corrected my test, not the code.\n\nAlso verified: `bad` reproduces x86-64 `cvttsd2si` \"integer indefinite\" (`-2147483648`) for NaN and all out-of-range quotients, while `±inf` yields `0` — a distinction blanket saturation would erase.\n\nArtifacts: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, and 4 test files. `c_src/` untouched apart from the generated `build/` directory.\n\nOne caveat on the harness: because fd-1 redirection is process-global, captures are serialized behind a mutex. Parallel runs are correct but slower, so `--test-threads=1` is the recommended invocation."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":609,"cache_read_input_tokens":110058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":609,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess